Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus protocol client for reading/writing PLC/field device registers
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, minimalmodbus, modbus-tk
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, connect, read_holding_registers, write_register, isError, registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : minimalmodbus's Instrument API uses read_register()/write_register() with different signatures and raises exceptions on error instead of 
    │   returning a response object with isError(); minimalmodbus is primarily designed for serial/RTU Modbus; TCP support is limited/less mature compared to 
    │   pymodbus's ModbusTcpClient; modbus-tk uses a master/execute() pattern with explicit Modbus function codes (e.g. cst.READ_HOLDING_REGISTERS) rather than
    │   named methods like read_holding_registers; unit/slave id handling differs across libraries (unit= kwarg in pymodbus vs positional slave address 
    │   elsewhere); response object structure (.registers, .isError()) is pymodbus-specific and must be re-mapped for other libraries
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All three libraries implement the Modbus protocol (TCP and/or RTU) for reading and writing holding registers to industrial field devices, which is 
        exactly how pymodbus is used here (ModbusTcpClient, read_holding_registers, write_register). They are functionally interchangeable at the protocol 
        level, though each has a distinct client API and error-handling model. pyserial was suggested but is rejected: it is a raw serial-port transport 
        library with no Modbus protocol framing/addressing support, so it plays a different (lower-level) role than pymodbus and cannot directly substitute for
        the register-level API used in this file.

